<TopicPage slug="subjects/finance/intermediate/diversification-and-portfolios">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Diversification is finance's only free lunch: <strong>spreading investments across imperfectly
    correlated assets reduces risk without reducing expected return</strong>. It works because
    assets don't all fail together — and its power depends entirely on correlation, which is why
    "own many things" and "own many different things" are different strategies.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the mathematics of not losing everything at once" widget="equationStep" config={divCfg} />

  <h2>The practical portfolio</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Broad index funds are pre-diversified:</strong> one S&P 500 fund holds 500 companies; a global fund, thousands. The default that beats most hand-picked portfolios on cost and behaviour alone.</li>
    <li><strong>Bonds are the ballast:</strong> historically less correlated with stocks (until inflation spikes — 2022 taught the exception). The stock/bond split is the portfolio's risk dial.</li>
    <li><strong>Over-diversification is real but rare:</strong> beyond ~20–30 uncorrelated holdings, benefit flattens while costs and complexity grow. "Diworsification" (Lynch) — owning everything, understanding nothing.</li>
    <li><strong>Correlations spike in crises:</strong> in 2008, nearly everything fell together. Diversification reduces everyday risk; it does not eliminate tail risk (the options lesson shows hedging that can).</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Markowitz (1952); Brinson, Hood & Beebower (1986); Bernstein, The Intelligent Asset Allocator.</p>
</TopicPage>
